{
  "name": "an owner takes the organisation fully remote, and coming back is reviewed again",
  "description": "ADR-0024 decision 15, and decision 13 a second time.\n\nAn organisation with an office may go fully remote. ONLY AN OWNER may — it changes what the organisation is, like the roster and the settings — and only while no DRAFT or OPEN role has an onsite entry: the refusal is `onsite_roles_exist` with `roles` naming them, and the owner closes or revises them first. A remote-only open role does not block it. The switch, in one transaction, stamps EVERY live address removed — the main office included, which this is the only path to remove — with `removed_by` the owner, and sets the kind. It is not reviewed and emails nobody: it takes capability away rather than granting any.\n\nClosed roles that named those addresses are untouched and still render them, marked removed. Afterwards the organisation is fully remote in every respect: no address can be added, no onsite entry written, and switching again is refused.\n\nCOMING BACK is decision 13 again, reviewed again: a member cannot ask, an owner can, and on approval the new address becomes the ONLY live main office. The old main office is not revived; it stays removed beside the new one, which is why the schema counts only live main offices.\n\nThe way back is rejected once before it is approved, and the MEMBER reads the rejection's reason from GET …/addresses (amendment 1 §1.3, the owner's decision): a member drafting a role can see why Onsite is unavailable without asking an owner what an email said. `roles` on `onsite_roles_exist` is `[{id, title, status}]`, oldest first (§1.4). The onsite entry refused after the switch points at an id nobody holds and answers `organization_fully_remote`, which §1.5 checks before the address.",
  "seed": ["principals"],
  "steps": [
    {
